<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Turicum</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Turicum was the Roman settlement on the site of modern Zürich, at the outflow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limmat">Limmat</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-zurich">Lake Zurich</a>. It held a customs station collecting the duty on goods passing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a>. In the late Roman period a fort stood on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindenhof">Lindenhof</a> hill, which remained the centre of the early medieval town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/turicum.jpg" alt="Turicum" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland zh · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weinplatz_-_Lindenhof_-_Schipfe_-_Limmatquai_2015-02-26_12-41-21.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Turicum lay at the lower end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-zurich">Lake Zurich</a>, in the Roman province of Germania Superior near its border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a>. Its core was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindenhof">Lindenhof</a>, a moraine hill in what is now the Altstadt of Zurich, about 428 metres above sea level and some 25 metres above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limmat">Limmat</a>. The settlement was bounded on the west by the line of the present Rennweg and Bahnhofstrasse and on the north by the slope towards the old delta of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sihl">Sihl</a>. Its importance came from the waterway running from the Walensee through Lake Zurich and down the Limmat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aare">Aare</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> pile dwellings stood on the marshy shore nearby, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> settled on and around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindenhof">Lindenhof</a> probably in the 1st century BCE or earlier. Finds of the La Tène period include lumps of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potin">Potin</a> coins, the largest weighing 59.2 kilograms and made of about 18,000 coins of around 100 BCE, which researchers now take to be cult offerings. The Roman vicus was founded probably around 15 BCE. It belonged first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-belgica">Gallia Belgica</a> and from 90 CE to Germania Superior.</p><p>The name is first attested on the tombstone of Lucius Aelius Urbicus, infant son of the head of the toll station, found on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindenhof">Lindenhof</a> in 1747 and dated to 185/200 CE. Turicum had no walls; its buildings grouped round the customs post, the Quadragesima Galliarum, which levied a duty of 2.5% where cargoes passed between lake ships and river boats. A harbour district grew at the foot of the Lindenhof in 70/75 CE, and baths of the 2nd to 4th centuries have been excavated near the Weinplatz. In 122 CE, under Emperor Hadrian, a round wooden temple was built on the island of Grosser Hafner.</p><p>According to Christian legend, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-and-regula">Felix and Regula</a> were executed at the site of the Wasserkirche in 286. Under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-i">Valentinian I</a> (364–375) the army built a fort of 4,500 square metres on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindenhof">Lindenhof</a> against the Alamanni, with ten towers and walls two metres thick. The Alamanni settled probably from the 5th century, when Roman troops withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, but the fort lasted into the 7th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Few remains of Roman Zurich have been excavated systematically, since they lie under the dense centre of the modern city and digs usually come with the renewal of buildings. Some finds are shown in place at the Thermengasse and in the Lindenhofkeller on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindenhof">Lindenhof</a> hill. The Lindenhof is listed as a Class A site of national importance in the Swiss inventory of cultural property.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Turicum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Romans kept a customs post there, <b>Turicum</b>, on the route between Gaul and Raetia, and a late Roman fort stood on the Lindenhof hill.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
